\pard\f0\fs30\sl360\slmult1\sa150 \b Abstract\b0\par 
\pard\f0\fs21\sl360\slmult1\sa150 Nurses' Medical Waste Management and Improvement Strategies Based on the Social Ecological Model\b0\par 
\pard\f0\fs21\sl360\slmult1\sa150 This study examined factors associated with nurses' medical waste management and explored clinical experiences using an explanatory sequential mixed methods design. Survey data from 181 nurses at three hospitals were analyzed, with 179 cases included in the final regression model. Three focus groups included 22 nurses. Qualitative content analysis linked meaning units to codes, subcategories, and categories. Findings were interpreted across social ecological levels and integrated with the quantitative results.\b0\par 
\pard\f0\fs21\sl360\slmult1\sa150 The mean medical waste management score was 3.90 out of 5, with a standard deviation of 0.61. The final model explained 54.7 percent of the variance. Green servant leadership, participation intention, environmental concern, nursing work intensity, and working in examination or procedure units were significant correlates. The outcome scale included self-reported practices and perceptions and did not directly measure segregation accuracy.\b0\par 
\pard\f0\fs21\sl360\slmult1\sa150 Qualitative analysis yielded 30 codes, 15 subcategories, and six categories: uncertainty in classification and safety-oriented judgments; differences in intention and difficulties sustaining practice; practice norms shaped by managers and colleagues; constraints imposed by workflow and the physical environment; management responsive to unit characteristics and coordination between occupational groups; and support through staff participation and ongoing improvement. Common difficulties were identified across hospitals, while specific experiences differed with clinical tasks and waste-handling conditions.\b0\par 
\pard\f0\fs21\sl360\slmult1\sa150 Integrated findings highlighted the need to support individual motivation through practical guidance, sustained managerial support, accessible disposal facilities, and coordinated collection. The positive adjusted association of work intensity differed from accounts of difficulty during busy periods and should not be interpreted as a benefit of greater workload. Case-based education and unit-specific improvements should be evaluated alongside safety and actual segregation performance.\b0\par 
\pard\f0\fs21\sl360\slmult1\sa150 Keywords: medical waste, nurses, social ecological model, focus groups, content analysis, mixed methods\b0\par 
